DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The ffeecclleeaarreexxcceepptt() routine clears the floating-point exception flags
     specified by _e_x_c_e_p_t_s, whereas ffeerraaiisseeeexxcceepptt() raises the specified excep‐
     tions.  Raising an exception causes the corresponding flag to be set, and
     a SIGFPE is delivered to the process if the exception is unmasked.

     The ffeetteesstteexxcceepptt() function determines which flags are currently set, of
     those specified by _e_x_c_e_p_t_s.

     The ffeeggeetteexxcceeppttffllaagg() function stores the state of the exception flags
     specified in _e_x_c_e_p_t_s in the opaque object pointed to by _f_l_a_g_p.  Simi‐
     larly, ffeesseetteexxcceeppttffllaagg() changes the specified exception flags to reflect
     the state stored in the object pointed to by _f_l_a_g_p.  Note that the flags
     restored with ffeesseetteexxcceeppttffllaagg() must be a (not necessarily proper) subset
     of the flags recorded by a prior call to ffeeggeetteexxcceeppttffllaagg().

     For all of these functions, the possible types of exceptions include
     those described in fenv(3).  Some architectures may define other types of
     floating-point exceptions.

IIMMPPLLEEMMEENNTTAATTIIOONN NNOOTTEESS
     On some architectures, raising an overflow or underflow exception also
     causes an inexact exception to be raised.  In these cases, the overflow
     or underflow will be raised first.

     The ffeeggeetteexxcceeppttffllaagg() and ffeesseetteexxcceeppttffllaagg() routines are preferred to
     ffeetteesstteexxcceepptt() and ffeerraaiisseeeexxcceepptt(), respectively, for saving and restor‐
     ing exception flags.  The latter do not re-raise exceptions and may pre‐
     serve architecture-specific information such as addresses where excep‐
     tions occurred.

RREETTUURRNN VVAALLUUEESS
     The ffeecclleeaarreexxcceepptt(), ffeeggeetteexxcceeppttffllaagg(), ffeerraaiisseeeexxcceepptt(), and
     ffeesseetteexxcceeppttffllaagg() functions return 0 upon success, and non-zero other‐
     wise.  The ffeetteesstteexxcceepptt() function returns the bitwise OR of the values
     of the current exception flags that were requested.

